 "cells": [
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-md-1",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "# Polyglot Programming\n\n> Verified against crush-ast `aa07413` (2026-10-16), with Python 3.14, Node 26 and\n> bash 5.3 on the host.\n\nA Crush program can embed code from other languages with `@language { ... }`\nblocks. This chapter describes what actually happens when you run one today —\nwhich languages work, how values cross the boundary, how errors and timeouts\nbehave, and, importantly, **how much isolation you do and don't get**.\n\n> **Status: alpha, and the security story is the part to read carefully.** By\n> default a polyglot block is an ordinary subprocess running with *your* user's\n> authority. An optional bubblewrap sandbox exists but is **off by default** and\n> not enabled by any command-line flag. See [Sandbox and authority](#sandbox-and-authority).\n\n## The model in one paragraph\n\nA block's source text is handed to a real interpreter — `python3 -c`, `node -e`,\nor `bash -c` — as a **child process**. Crush marshals some variables in, runs the\ninterpreter, captures its standard output, and (for Python and JavaScript)\nmarshals back the variables the block **declares** with `-> names`. There is no embedded WebAssembly runtime, no\nWASI capability bridge, and no in-process interpreter.\n\n## Languages\n\n| Block | Runs | Aliases |\n|---|---|---|\n| `@python { }` | `python3 -c` | `@python3`, `@py` |\n| `@javascript { }` | `node -e` | `@js`, `@node`, `@es6`, `@ecmascript` |\n| `@bash { }` | `bash -c` | `@sh` |\n\nThe interpreter must be on `PATH`; `crush doctor` checks which ones it finds. Any\nother tag (`@rust`, `@go`, `@c`, …) parses, but the program is refused before it\nruns (`not available in this host: polyglot.rust`).\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "done"
   },
   "meta": {},
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-1",
   "kind": {
    "type": "crush"
   },
   "source": "@rust {\n    fn main() {}\n}\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "pending"
   },
   "meta": {
    "tags": [
     "guide",
     "needs-host"
    ],
    "extra": {
     "guide": {
      "src": "crush/polyglot.md:38",
      "browser": "host",
      "why": "uses `@rust` blocks (polyglot), which the in-browser runtime does not provide",
      "expect_error": "not available in this host: polyglot.rust"
     }
    }
   },
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-md-3",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "(crush-ast also ships **walkers** for Rust, Go, C, Zig, Wasm and others. A walker\ntranslates a *source file* into CAST for compilation — it is a separate\nmechanism, driven by the `crush-walker` tool, and is not what an `@lang { }`\nblock does.)\n\n## Enabling polyglot execution\n\nPolyglot is **off by default**, because running another interpreter is full\nambient authority. Turn it on per run:\n\n```sh\ncrush run --polyglot program.crush        # same flag on crush-run\n```\n\nWithout it the program is refused before anything runs (`--polyglot:\npolyglot.python`):\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "done"
   },
   "meta": {},
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-2",
   "kind": {
    "type": "crush"
   },
   "source": "fn main() {\n    @python {\n    print(\"hi\")\n    }\n}\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "pending"
   },
   "meta": {
    "tags": [
     "guide",
     "expected-failure"
    ],
    "extra": {
     "guide": {
      "src": "crush/polyglot.md:62",
      "browser": "fail",
      "why": "this example shows a refusal",
      "expect_error": "polyglot.python"
     }
    }
   },
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-md-5",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "An embedding host enables it explicitly with\n`HostCapsBuilder::polyglot(&[\"python\", \"javascript\", \"bash\"])`; each language is\ngated by its own capability name (`polyglot.python`, `polyglot.javascript`,\n`polyglot.bash`).\n\n## Output\n\nWhatever the block writes to standard output appears in the program's output, in\norder. Two details to know: the block's output is **trimmed** (so it carries no\ntrailing newline), and anything written to standard error is discarded when the\nblock succeeds.\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "done"
   },
   "meta": {},
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-3",
   "kind": {
    "type": "crush"
   },
   "source": "print(\"one\")\n@python {\nprint(\"two\")\n}\nprint(\"three\")\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "pending"
   },
   "meta": {
    "tags": [
     "guide",
     "needs-host"
    ],
    "extra": {
     "guide": {
      "src": "crush/polyglot.md:81",
      "browser": "host",
      "why": "uses `@python` blocks (polyglot), which the in-browser runtime does not provide",
      "expect_output": "one\ntwothree\n"
     }
    }
   },
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-md-7",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "If you want the following output on a new line, end the block's output with an\nexplicit blank line (`print()`), or print from Crush.\n\n## Passing values in and out\n\n### Python and JavaScript\n\nFor `@python` and `@javascript` blocks:\n\n- **In (automatic):** every name the block *reads* that is a Crush variable\n  already in scope (a `let`, a parameter, a loop variable, or an earlier block's\n  output) is injected as a variable of that name, with its type preserved.\n- **Out (declared):** the names after `->` in the block header are marshaled back\n  and become (or assign) Crush variables at that line. Nothing else leaves the\n  block. Use `-> name` for one and `-> (a, b)` for several.\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "done"
   },
   "meta": {},
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-4",
   "kind": {
    "type": "crush"
   },
   "source": "let count = 10\nlet base = 5\n@python -> total {\ntmp = base * 3\ntotal = tmp * 2\n}\nprint(total)\nprint(count)\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "pending"
   },
   "meta": {
    "tags": [
     "guide",
     "needs-host"
    ],
    "extra": {
     "guide": {
      "src": "crush/polyglot.md:112",
      "browser": "host",
      "why": "uses `@python` blocks (polyglot), which the in-browser runtime does not provide",
      "expect_output": "30\n10\n"
     }
    }
   },
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-md-9",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "Here `base` goes in, `total` comes out, and `tmp` stays inside the block (using it\nafterwards is a compile error, `Undefined variable`). JavaScript works the same\nway; values travel as JSON:\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "done"
   },
   "meta": {},
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-5",
