  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-md-23",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "Python and JavaScript state does **not** persist between blocks: each block is a\nfresh interpreter, and only the declared outputs carry over. You cannot define a\nfunction in one block and call it from Crush or from another block.\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "done"
   },
   "meta": {},
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-12",
   "kind": {
    "type": "crush"
   },
   "source": "@python -> a {\na = 1\nhidden = 2\n}\n@python -> r {\nr = \"hidden\" in globals()\n}\nprint(a)\nprint(r)\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "pending"
   },
   "meta": {
    "tags": [
     "guide",
     "needs-host"
    ],
    "extra": {
     "guide": {
      "src": "crush/polyglot.md:286",
      "browser": "host",
      "why": "uses `@python` blocks (polyglot), which the in-browser runtime does not provide",
      "expect_output": "1\nfalse\n"
     }
    }
   },
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-md-25",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "### Imports for blocks: `use @lang`\n\n`use @lang python \"module\"` (optionally `as alias`) writes the import into every\nlater `@python` block, so each block doesn't have to repeat it. Each block is\nstill its own process:\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "done"
   },
   "meta": {},
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-13",
   "kind": {
    "type": "crush"
   },
   "source": "use @lang python \"math\"\nuse @lang python \"json\" as j\n@python -> (val, text) {\nval = math.factorial(5)\ntext = j.dumps({\"a\": 1})\n}\nprint(val)\nprint(text)\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "pending"
   },
   "meta": {
    "tags": [
     "guide",
     "needs-host"
    ],
    "extra": {
     "guide": {
      "src": "crush/polyglot.md:311",
      "browser": "host",
      "why": "uses `@python` blocks (polyglot), which the in-browser runtime does not provide",
      "expect_output": "120\n{\"a\": 1}\n"
     }
    }
   },
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-md-27",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "### Bash\n\n`@bash` blocks have no value marshaling: only their printed output comes back,\nand `->` on a `@bash` block is a compile error (`` `->` output lists are not\nsupported for @bash: it has no value marshaling ``).\n\n> **Known bug ([crush-ast #255](https://github.com/nixpt/crush-ast/issues/255)).**\n> Earlier releases passed the function's top-level `let`s to a `@bash` block as\n> environment variables. Since explicit outputs landed (CRUSH-270) a `@bash` block\n> receives **no** Crush variables, and `$name` silently expands to the empty\n> string:\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "done"
   },
   "meta": {},
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-14",
   "kind": {
    "type": "crush"
   },
   "source": "let name = \"crush\"\n@bash {\necho \"hello [$name]\"\n}\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "pending"
   },
   "meta": {
    "tags": [
     "guide",
     "needs-host"
    ],
    "extra": {
     "guide": {
      "src": "crush/polyglot.md:341",
      "browser": "host",
      "why": "uses `@bash` blocks (polyglot), which the in-browser runtime does not provide",
      "expect_output": "hello []\n"
     }
    }
   },
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-md-29",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "Until that is fixed, compute the value in a `@python` block and print it, or\nwrite it to a file the `@bash` block reads. Bash inherits the host environment\n(`$HOME`, `$PATH`, …) in the default, unsandboxed lane.\n\n## Errors\n\nIf the interpreter exits non-zero, the program stops with a\n`LangRuntimeError`. The message is the guest's own stderr, prefixed with the\n**Crush source line of the block**, so a Python traceback is attributed to the\nright place in your `.crush` file:\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "done"
   },
   "meta": {},
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-15",
   "kind": {
    "type": "crush"
   },
   "source": "print(\"a\")\nprint(\"b\")\n@python {\nraise ValueError(\"x\")\n}\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "pending"
   },
   "meta": {
    "tags": [
     "guide",
     "needs-host"
    ],
    "extra": {
     "guide": {
      "src": "crush/polyglot.md:366",
      "browser": "host",
      "why": "uses `@python` blocks (polyglot), which the in-browser runtime does not provide",
      "expect_error": "at .crush line 3"
     }
    }
   },
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-md-31",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "```text\n[runtime] @python block raised a runtime error: (at .crush line 3) Traceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"<string>\", line 2, in <module>\n    raise ValueError(\"x\")\nValueError: x\n```\n\nA guest failure is **not** catchable with Crush `try`/`catch`\n([Control Flow](https://docs.crushlang.org/crush/control_flow.html#what-catch-does-not-catch)). Handle expected\nfailures inside the block (`try:` / `except:` in Python) and return a value that\nsays what happened. Failures to *start* a sandboxed block — package resolution,\nmissing `bwrap` — are reported separately as `sandbox setup failed`, so a\nprovisioning problem is never mistaken for a bug in your code.\n\n## Timeouts\n\nEvery block runs under a **wall-clock limit of 30 seconds** (`Quotas::max_wall_time_ms`,\ndefault `30_000`). At the deadline the interpreter's whole process group is killed\nand the program ends with:\n\n```text\n[runtime] 'polyglot.python' exceeded its 30000ms wall-clock quota and was killed\n```\n\n(crush-ast CRUSH-19.) The limit is a field of `Quotas`, so it is configurable\nwhen you embed the VM; `crush-run` doesn't expose a flag for it. In the sandboxed\nlane the limit also covers package provisioning. The instruction quota\n(`--max-steps`) does not apply to time spent inside a guest.\n\n## Sandbox and authority\n\n**Default (no sandbox).** A polyglot block is a plain child process of the Crush\nruntime and inherits its environment, working directory, user and network. It is\n**not** subject to the capability flags in [Capability System](https://docs.crushlang.org/crush/capabilities.html):\n`--fs-root` does not confine it, and withholding `--net` or `--process` does not\nstop a block from opening sockets or running commands. This was verified: with\nonly `--polyglot`, a `@python` block reads `/etc/hostname`, and a `@bash` block\nruns `id` and `cat`.\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "done"
   },
   "meta": {},
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-16",
   "kind": {
    "type": "crush"
   },
   "source": "@python {\nhost = open(\"/etc/hostname\").read().strip()\n}\nprint(len(host) > 0)\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "pending"
   },
   "meta": {
    "tags": [
     "guide",
     "needs-host"
    ],
    "extra": {
     "guide": {
      "src": "crush/polyglot.md:414",
      "browser": "host",
      "why": "uses `@python` blocks (polyglot), which the in-browser runtime does not provide",
      "expect_output": "true\n"
     }
    }
   },
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-md-33",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "Only use `--polyglot` on programs you trust, in an environment you are prepared\nto lose.\n\n**Sandboxed lane (opt-in at build time).** crush-ast can instead provision the\nlanguage runtime with [`buckets`](https://github.com/nixpt/buckets) and run the\nblock under `bwrap` (bubblewrap) — CRUSH-20, with registry dependencies added in\nCRUSH-66. It is gated behind the `sandboxed-polyglot` Cargo feature of `crush-vm`,\nwhich is **off by default** and **not re-exported as a `crush-lang-sdk` feature**\n(so there is no `--features` shortcut and no CLI flag). You enable it by building\nthe workspace with `--features crush-vm/sandboxed-polyglot`. It needs `bwrap`\ninstalled and, on first use, network access to download the runtime.\n\nWhat that lane does (verified against crush-ast `4e9c388`):\n\n| | Default | Sandboxed |\n|---|---|---|\n| interpreter | host's `python3` / `node` / `bash` | pinned `python@3.11`, `node@20`, `bash@5` from buckets |\n| host files (`/etc/hostname`) | readable | not visible (`FileNotFoundError`) |\n| network | inherited | **blocked** (`allow_network: false`) |\n| working directory | inherited | bound **read-write** |\n| `@lang[deps]` | ignored | resolved and bound read-only |\n\nDependencies go in square brackets after the language tag. Registry specs are\nprefixed `pypi:` or `npm:`; bare names are buckets package aliases. A bare\n`numpy` is **not** assumed to mean PyPI.\n\n```crush\n@python[pypi:six] -> v {\nimport six\nv = six.__version__\n}\nprint(v)\n```\n\n```text\n1.17.0\n```\n\nThe first run prints buckets' progress lines (`resolved python.org@^3.11 → …`) to\nstandard error while it downloads. In a default build the `[...]` part still\nparses, but the dependencies are **silently ignored**: `import six` then works\nonly if the host's own Python happens to have `six`, and an unresolvable\n`[pypi:nothing_here_xyz]` is not reported at all. An unknown package fails with\n`@python block sandbox setup failed: Failed to resolve version for 'pypi:…'`.\n\nEven the sandboxed lane is a first cut: the working directory is writable, memory\nis not limited, and only `python`, `javascript` and `bash` are wired up.\n\n## Limitations summary\n\n- Languages: Python, JavaScript (Node), Bash. Nothing else executes.\n- Value passing is JSON-only, Python/JS only, and outputs are declared with `->`.\n- No shared interpreter state or cross-block function calls.\n- Guest errors and timeouts abort the program; they can't be caught from Crush.\n- Default execution is **unsandboxed**; the sandbox is a build-time opt-in.\n\n## Next Steps\n\n- **[Capability System](https://docs.crushlang.org/crush/capabilities.html)**: what the flags do and don't confine\n- **[Standard Library](https://docs.crushlang.org/crush/stdlib.html)**\n- **[CAST Specification](https://docs.crushlang.org/cast/index.html)**\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "done"
   },
   "meta": {},
   "outputs": []
  }
 ]
}
